<article class="env-lemma" id="0B2Q">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0B2Q">Lemma <span data-tag="0B2Q">33.34.2</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field and $n \geq 0$. Let $X, Y \subset \mathbf{A}^ n_ k$ be closed subsets. Assume that $X$ and $Y$ are equidimensional, $\dim (X) = r$ and $\dim (Y) = s$. Then every irreducible component of $X \cap Y$ has dimension $\geq r + s - n$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Consider the closed subscheme $X \times Y \subset \mathbf{A}^{2n}_ k$ where we use coordinates $x_1, \ldots , x_ n, y_1, \ldots , y_ n$. Then $X \cap Y = X \times Y \cap V(x_1 - y_1, \ldots , x_ n - y_ n)$. Let $t \in X \cap Y \subset X \times Y$ be a closed point. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0B2M" data-tag="0B2M">33.20.5</a> we have $\dim _ t(X \times Y) = \dim (X) + \dim (Y)$. Thus $\dim (\mathcal{O}_{X \times Y, t}) = r + s$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0A21" data-tag="0A21">33.20.3</a>. By Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00KW" data-tag="00KW">10.60.13</a> we conclude that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \dim (\mathcal{O}_{X \cap Y, t}) = \dim (\mathcal{O}_{X \times Y, t}/(x_1 - y_1, \ldots , x_ n - y_ n)) \geq r + s - n  \]
</div>
    <p>
       This implies the result by Lemma <a href="/tag/0A21" data-tag="0A21">33.20.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>